WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 49: Jesse James and the Fall of the Outlaw Empire

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.920
[Rebecca] In the aftermath of the Civil War, the state of Missouri was a fractured, bitter borderland scarred by savage guerrilla warfare.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.800
[Rebecca] Neighbors had murdered neighbors, and the surrender at Appomattox did not bring peace.

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:27.520
[Rebecca] Out of this cauldron of blood and resentment emerged the most famous and romanticized bandit in American history, Jesse Woodson James.

6
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:38.720
[Rebecca] For 16 years, Jesse and his brother Frank led the James Younger gang in an audacious string of daytime bank heists, stagecoach robberies, and the world's first daylight train holdups.

7
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:48.960
[Rebecca] Sympathetic Southern sympathizers and sensational dime novelists cast Jesse as an American Robin Hood fighting against northern banks and railroad monopolies.

8
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:59.760
[Rebecca] But behind the myth was a cold, ruthless guerrilla fighter, whose empire unraveled in a hail of gunfire in Minnesota, leading to his treacherous murder by one of his own men.

9
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.400
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:20.720
[George] In the 1860s, Western Missouri's Clay County was known as Little Dixie, a prosperous slave-holding agricultural enclave surrounded by pro-union territory.

11
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:26.800
[George] When the Civil War erupted, Missouri became a lawless bloodbath of irregular partisan warfare.

12
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:36.640
[George] Jesse's older brother Frank joined William Clark Quantrill's notorious Confederate guerrilla raiders, taking part in the infamous 1863 Lawrence Massacre.

13
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:49.520
[George] When Union militia raided the James family farm in Kearney, they tied up 16-year-old Jesse, whipped him with horse halters, and hanged his stepfather from a tree until he was near death to extract information.

14
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:56.720
[George] Enraged and radicalized, 16-year old Jesse joined Bloody Bill Anderson's savage guerrilla unit.

15
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:05.960
[George] Jesse became a lethal, hardened combatant, learning to ride at a full gallop with his horse's reins between his teeth while firing a Colt revolver in each hand.

16
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:14.120
[George] When the Confederacy surrendered in 1865, Jesse was shot through the chest while attempting to surrender under a white flag.

17
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:21.560
[George] Embittered, impoverished, and nursing an unquenchable hatred for the Union government, Jesse refused to accept defeat.

18
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:32.840
[George] Jesse and Frank James were forged in the brutal guerrilla crucible of quantril raiders and bloody Bill Anderson, fighting a savage civil war without mercy along the Missouri-Kansas border.

19
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:51.560
[George] On February 13, 1866, in the icy streets of Liberty, Missouri, a dozen heavily armed men rode into town in broad daylight, surrounded the Clay County Savings Association, and pulled off the first successful daylight peacetime bank robbery in United States history.

20
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:59.320
[George] Escaping with $60,000 in gold and greenbacks, the bandits fatally shot an innocent college student in the street.

21
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:08.280
[George] Thus began the reign of the James Younger gang, composed of Frank and Jesse James, alongside their cousins Cole, Jim, and Bob Younger.

22
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.080
[George] The gang moved with military precision.

23
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:25.160
[George] They used relay teams of fresh horses hidden along getaway routes, wore heavy linen dusters to conceal their pistols, and relied on a vast, loyal network of Confederate sympathizers across Missouri who sheltered them in barns and cellars.

24
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:31.400
[George] In 1873, near Adair, Iowa, the gang achieved another historic milestone.

25
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:39.240
[George] Using a rope to pull a rail loose on the Chicago, Rock Island, and Pacific Line, they caused a passenger train to derail.

26
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Robbing the safe in the baggage car, the first daylight train robbery in the American West.

27
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:57.280
[George] Following the war, the James Younger gang turned their guerrilla tactics against banks, express trains, and federal payrolls, using fast thoroughbred horses and Colt 44s to evade pursuit.

28
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:11.040
[George] Jesse James was a master of self-promotion, but his transformation from a ruthless bandit into an immortal folk hero was the creation of a brilliant journalist, John Newman Edwards, editor of the Kansas City Times.

29
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:24.560
[George] Edwards was an unrepentant Confederate romantic who viewed Jesse as a heroic Southern cavalier waging a righteous resistance against northern carpetbaggers, corrupt Republican banks, and predatory railroad monopolies.

30
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:38.000
[George] Edwards published lavish, poetic editorials and published personal letters written by Jesse himself, portraying the outlaws as modern-day Robin Hoods who only robbed wealthy corporations and gave money to poor widows.

31
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:47.200
[George] The myth took deep root across the rural South and Midwest, where struggling farmers despised the railroads for charging exorbitant grain shipping rates.

32
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:59.920
[George] In reality, there is not a single documented record of Jesse James ever sharing a single stolen penny with anyone outside his own gang, but the romantic legend shielded him from arrest for over a decade.

33
00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] Confederate sympathizer John Newman Edwards wrote romanticized editorials in the Kansas City Times, casting Jesse as a modern Robin Hood defending Southern honor against Northern carpetbaggers.

34
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:24.720
[George] Desperate to end the gang's reign of terror, Midwestern Railroad Corporations hired the premier private detective agency in the world, the Pinkerton National Detective Agency, founded by Alan Pinkerton.

35
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:31.960
[George] Pinkerton declared personal war on the James Brothers, dispatching dozens of undercover operatives into western Missouri.

36
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.000
[George] But Jesse and Frank were seasoned combat scouts.

37
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.280
[George] They sniffed out the detectives, ambushed them on country roads, and executed them.

38
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:49.560
[George] Enraged by the humiliation, Alan Pinkerton orchestrated a covert raid on the James family homestead in Kearney in January 1875.

39
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:55.720
[George] Detectives surrounded the cabin in the dead of night and hurled an iron incendiary flare through the window.

40
00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:09.000
[George] When the device rolled into the fireplace, it exploded like a bomb, blowing off the right arm of Jesse's mother, Zerilda Samuel, and fatally shrapneling Jesse's eight-year-old half-brother, Archie, through the chest.

41
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.720
[George] Jesse and Frank were not even in the house.

42
00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:23.720
[George] The botched, cowardly bombing horrified the nation, creating immense public sympathy for the James brothers and forcing the Missouri State Legislature to debate granting the outlaws full legal amnesty.

43
00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:37.800
[George] Pinkerton detectives tossed an incendiary iron bomb into the James family home in Clay County, killing Jesse's eight-year-old half-brother Archie and blowing off his mother's right arm, galvanizing public sympathy for the outlaws.

44
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:48.040
[George] Flush with arrogance and seeking their largest score yet, the James Younger gang rode far north of their familiar Missouri safe haven in September 1876.

45
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:58.360
[George] On the afternoon of September 7, eight heavily armed gang members rode into the quiet college town of Northfield, Minnesota, targeting the first national bank.

46
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.800
[George] But the Northfield raid was a catastrophic disaster.

47
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:10.520
[George] Inside the bank, heroic cashier Joseph Lee Haywood refused to open the steel safe, even when Jesse pressed a revolver to his temple.

48
00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.840
[George] The outlaws murdered Haywood, but escaped with less than $30.

49
00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.880
[George] Outside, the citizens of Northfield refused to cower.

50
00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:31.760
[George] Local hardware merchants broke open crates of rifles, and ordinary townspeople took up sniper positions on rooftops and street corners, pouring a devastating volley of rifle fire into the mounted bandits.

51
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:39.360
[George] In a brutal ten-minute gun battle, two bandits were killed in the street, and all three younger brothers were severely wounded.

52
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:46.000
[George] Frank and Jesse fled across the prairie, while the youngers were hunted down and captured in a swamp two weeks later.

53
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.760
[George] The invincible James Younger gang was permanently shattered.

54
00:07:49.760 --> 00:08:02.800
[George] In September 1876, the gang attempted to rob the first national bank in Northfield, Minnesota, but armed citizens opened fire from storefronts, annihilating the gang in a furious street battle.

55
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:10.320
[George] For five years following the Northfield disaster, Frank and Jesse lived under assumed names in Tennessee and Missouri.

56
00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.120
[George] But Jesse could not abandon the outlaw life.

57
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.760
[George] He recruited a new, second-rate gang of petty criminals to rob trains.

58
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.480
[George] By 1882, 34-year-old Jesse was living quietly in St.

59
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:31.840
[George] Joseph, Missouri, under the alias of Thomas Howard, residing in a small white frame house on a hilltop with his wife Zee and their two young children.

60
00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:39.440
[George] Feeling paranoid and betrayed, Jesse trusted only two new young recruits, brothers Charlie and Robert Ford.

61
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:52.640
[George] Unknown to Jesse, 20-year-old Bob Ford had struck a secret, treasonous bargain with Missouri Governor Thomas Crittenden, who had placed a massive $10,000 bounty on Jesse's head, dead or alive.

62
00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:59.840
[George] On the warm morning of Monday, April 3, 1882, Jesse ate breakfast with the Fords in his home.

63
00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:11.640
[George] Unbuckling his gun belt and laying his revolvers on the bed, remarking that it was too warm to wear them, Jesse stepped onto a chair to dust a needlepoint picture of a horse hanging on the parlor wall.

64
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:21.320
[George] Seeing his back turned, Bob Ford drew a nickel-plated Colt 45 revolver and shot Jesse James through the back of the head, killing him instantly.

65
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:30.280
[George] On April 3, 1882, 20-year-old Bob Ford stood behind Jesse James as the outlaw adjusted a picture frame on the wall of his St.

66
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:35.000
[George] Joseph home, shooting him in the back of the head for a $10,000 reward.

67
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.880
[George] The treacherous assassination of Jesse James shocked and disgusted the nation.

68
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:54.280
[George] Although Governor Crittenden immediately pardoned the Ford brothers and paid them a fraction of the reward, the public treated Bob Ford with unmitigated contempt, forever branding him in American balladry as that dirty little coward who shot Mr.

69
00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.480
[George] Howard and laid poor Jesse in his grave.

70
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:11.720
[George] Ford capitalized on his notoriety by appearing in traveling stage shows reenacting the murder, but he was universally despised, and in 1892, he was shot dead in a Colorado tent saloon by an admirer of Jesse James.

71
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:25.480
[George] Jesse was buried in the yard of his Carney family farm under a granite headstone erected by his mother, which read, In loving memory of my beloved son, murdered by a traitor and coward whose name is not worthy to appear here.

72
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.280
[George] Today, the Jesse James home in St.

73
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:34.520
[George] Joseph remains a popular museum, where the fatal bullet hole in the wall is still preserved.

74
00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:47.440
[George] Jesse James remains an indelible symbol of the violent, divided heart of Frontier America, a man of blood and iron who terrorized an age, and whose legend outlived the nation that hunted him down.

75
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:57.680
[Rebecca] Jesse James and his legendary Outlaw Empire stand as an enduring chronicle of the bitter divisions and romantic myths that shape the post Civil War American frontier.

76
00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:04.400
[Rebecca] It shows that legends are often born in blood, sustained by myth, and remembered long after the guns fall silent.

77
00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:07.280
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

78
00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.480
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.200
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we continue our weekly journey across the American saga.

80
00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.680
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

